mtd: core: an OTP area that doesn't read is not fatal

mtd_otp_nvmem_add() fails the whole mtd_device_register() on any OTP read
error but -EOPNOTSUPP. The N810's OneNAND OTP area (where the CAL's
factory blocks live: the unit's WLAN MAC and BT address) reads with an
ECC error, so with CONFIG_MTD_ONENAND_OTP the OneNAND probe failed, the
root partition was gone, and the kernel panicked into a boot loop:

  onenand_wait: ECC error = 0x0008
  omap2-onenand 4000000.onenand: error -EBADMSG: Failed to register OTP NVMEM device
  ...
  Kernel panic - not syncing: VFS: Unable to mount root fs

The NVMEM devices are an extra; warn and go on without them (still
failing on -EPROBE_DEFER). Userspace OTP reads through mtdchar
(OTPSELECT) are a separate path and hand back the data despite ECC
errors.

--- a/drivers/mtd/mtdcore.c
+++ b/drivers/mtd/mtdcore.c
@@ -1082,10 +1082,19 @@
 
 err:
 	nvmem_unregister(mtd->otp_user_nvmem);
+	mtd->otp_user_nvmem = NULL;
 	/* Don't report error if OTP is not supported. */
 	if (err == -EOPNOTSUPP)
 		return 0;
-	return dev_err_probe(dev, err, "Failed to register OTP NVMEM device\n");
+	if (err == -EPROBE_DEFER)
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, err, "Failed to register OTP NVMEM device\n");
+	/*
+	 * The OTP NVMEM devices are an extra: an OTP area that doesn't read
+	 * (an ECC error in the N810 OneNAND's, as Nokia programmed it) must not
+	 * take the whole device and its partitions down with it.
+	 */
+	dev_warn(dev, "no OTP NVMEM device: %pe\n", ERR_PTR(err));
+	return 0;
 }
 
